As the owner I want every Claude call costed and counted against a monthly ceiling, so that the household never spends more than I set.

**Where.**

* `src/mypaisa_agent/agent.py`, `runs.py` and the Anthropic client wrapper;
* the `BudgetSettings` and `SpendMonth` items;
* `ARCHITECTURE.md` §15.1 and §15.2.

**Acceptance.**

* Every model call's cost is worked out in dollars from the API's own `usage` counts and the price table. That includes cache reads and writes, thinking billed as output, web searches, and Batches at half price.
* The cost is added atomically to the cycle's `SpendMonth` row, split by member, feature, agent and model. Pounds are shown beside it at the day's ECB rate.
* Before every call, the cycle's spend is read. At the last step (100% by default) no call is made. The card goes out as its plain template, and a typed message gets one line saying when free text is back.
* `BudgetSettings` is seeded with the defaults, each one a setting the admin changes in the Admin center (MA-116, MA-137):

    * prod ceiling $160 and dev $40, the Max 20x plan's $200 credit;
    * steps at 70%, 85%, 95% and 100%;
    * member share 60%;
    * daily guard at three times the daily average;
    * eight calls per run;
    * cycle start day 1;
    * the eval caps: prod's overnight judges at 3% of the ceiling; the evals workspace's live evals at $10 a run and $30 a month (ARCHITECTURE 23.7).

* The cycle starts on the day the plan's credit renews.
* The guards apply as set.
* The overnight judges are metered as the feature `evals`, kept to their 3%, and pause from the 85% step (MA-252).
* Every step sends an alert through the router (MA-137).
* Tests cover:

    * the cost of recorded `usage` blocks, to the cent;
    * a concurrent add;
    * the last-step stop;
    * each guard;
    * custom steps;
    * a cycle rollover on the renewal day;
    * the judges stopping at their share and at the 85% step.

* Gates: ruff, pyright, pytest.

**Depends on.** MA-21 (workspaces and limits), MA-112.

Updated 28 Oct 2026 (MA-245, review round 1): the `evals` feature and the eval caps.

As the owner I want to see this month's Claude spend and move the ceiling from the Admin center, so that I can allow more in a busy month or less in a quiet one without touching AWS or code.

**Where.** The Admin center in both doors: `ios/MyPaisa/` (the iOS app) and `web/` (the web app, MA-184); the `api` Lambda's `/v1/admin/budget` routes, `BudgetSettings`, `ARCHITECTURE.md` §15.1 and §15.2. Designed first (`.claude/skills/design-first`): 2 or 3 options in the MyPaisa iOS v1 project (`Spend and the ceiling`, `Move the ceiling` in `iPhone admin.dc.html`), and the web app from its picked browser boards in MyPaisa app v1; Arun picks. MA-137 adds the AWS caps, the steps and the alert routing to the same section.

**Acceptance.**

* The Admin center's Spend section, in the iOS app and in the web app, shows:

    * this cycle's spend against the ceiling, in dollars with pounds beside it, by member, feature, agent and model;
    * the current step, the days left, and the hard limit;
    * the Max 20x plan's $200 credit, shared by prod and dev.

* The owner can set:

    * the ceiling, from $10 up to the hard limit (defaults $160 prod, $40 dev);
    * the member share;
    * the renewal day;
    * the hard limit, typed in to match the Console workspace limit.

* A ceiling that would take prod and dev together past the credit warns that the extra needs a card, and needs an explicit yes.
* Every change needs the second check (MA-76): in the iOS app a fresh Sign in with Apple or passkey under Face ID (a member who signs in only with Google proves it with Google again), in the web app a passkey or a fresh sign-in in the browser. Each writes an audit row and sends the owner a message in the chat and a push. It takes effect on the next model call.
* Lowering the ceiling below what is already spent says, before Confirm, which step MyPaisa drops to.
* A ceiling above the hard limit is refused, with the steps to raise the Console limit first.
* From phase 18, the same changes can also be made from the admin's chat through the admin desk (MA-130).
* The admin only: the owner, the household's one admin (Arun, 25 Oct 2026: "there is only 1 admin"). Members can ask the chat how much has been spent (`get_spend`), but cannot change anything.
* Tests:

    * each route against each role;
    * the second check, from the app and from the browser;
    * the audit row;
    * the refusal above the hard limit;
    * the warning past the credit;
    * snapshot tests in both themes at iPhone and iPad sizes, and Playwright screenshots of the web page at phone and laptop widths.

* Gates: ruff, pyright, pytest; the iOS gates (MA-166); Playwright.

**Depends on.** MA-102, MA-113, MA-76.

Updated 25 Oct 2026 (MA-161): the Admin center is in the iOS app and, after Arun's correction, still in the web app (MA-184); the fresh check is the second check (MA-76); Telegram messages become the chat and push.

As the developer I want the Forecaster's design, bounds and phase written down, so that its stories build on one plan.

**Where.** `ARCHITECTURE.md` (new §19, the `Forecast` and `ModelVersion` items, §16, §17), `PLAN.md` (phase 11, the timeline, the risks), `TECH_STACK.md` (one row).

**Acceptance.**

* §19 records:

    * what can and cannot be forecast;
    * the point-in-time data;
    * the five models;
    * the strategy layer;
    * how the Forecaster proves itself and switches itself off;
    * the Telegram card and the web page;
    * where it runs and what it costs.

* PLAN phase 11 lists F1 to F7 with their keys, tests and exit, and F1 and F2 may start after phase 2.
