As the owner I want the chance each holding is up in four weeks, its likely range and what drives it, with a confidence that means what it says.

**Where.** `src/mypaisa_agent/forecaster/models/direction.py`, `ensemble.py`; the model registry.

**Acceptance.**

* **The forecast:**

    * a gradient-boosted model gives the chance of being up over 20 trading days;
    * the likely range (10th, 50th and 90th percentiles) comes from conformal intervals;
    * it is combined with the mood, risk and similar-days signals, then calibrated;
    * the top drivers behind each forecast are shown.

* **Validation:**

    * walk-forward tests leave a gap so no training window overlaps a test window;
    * the last two years are held out for a final test;
    * the forecast must beat the base rate on Brier score and log loss in the held-out years, or it is not used.

* **Model upkeep:**

    * model versions are stored in S3 with a `ModelVersion` row;
    * models are retrained monthly;
    * drift is watched.

* **Switch-off:** if the live Brier score is worse than the base rate for four weeks, the model switches itself off and the owner is told.
* Tests:

    * no feature is used before its known date;
    * calibration;
    * the switch-off.

* Gates: ruff, pyright, pytest.

**Depends on.** F3, F4.

As the owner I want the Forecaster's view next to the rules engine's call, in the chat and on a Forecast screen, so that I can compare them and see when they disagree.

**Where.** `cards/` (the card's JSON, MA-174), `ios/MyPaisa/` and `web/` (a Forecast screen per holding, with its proof page), the `api` routes. Designed first (`.claude/skills/design-first`): the app's screens are MA-281's 1a, "the answer first", which Arun picked on 29 Oct 2026. They are in Claude Design, MyPaisa iOS v1: iPhone v4 · Forecast (1a) and iPhone v4 · Forecaster proof and chat. The web app's Forecast is redrawn to MA-281's 1a in MA-279; until then its browser boards (Forecast.dc.html) stay its web spec.

**Acceptance.**

* **In the chat:** a side-by-side card for each holding, with:

    * the rules engine's call and confidence;
    * the Forecaster's chance of a fall over 5%, chance up, likely range, market mood, similar days, the pattern F8 found (MA-280, only where it shows an edge) and main drivers;
    * \[Why?\], \[Chart\] and \[Track record\] buttons.
    * When the two engines disagree, the card says so in words.

* **On the dashboard:** a Forecast screen per holding, in the iOS app (iPhone, iPad and the foldable) and in the web app (anything new is created in the chat; an existing figure can be corrected in place). As 1a draws it:

    * the answer first: the chance of a fall over 5%, the likely range, the chance of being up, with the rules engine's call under them;
    * candles for ETFs and shares with the fan of the likely range and the patterns F8 finds marked on them; a fund draws its daily price line with line patterns only;
    * one row per model, each opening its sheet: the market mood's timeline, the similar days drawn over today's chart, the event-impact table, the main drivers, the technical set;
    * both engines' track records side by side.

* **The proof page** (Arun kept it, 29 Oct 2026; ARCHITECTURE 19.7): reached from the Forecast tab and the Track record. It shows the lab's report, the walk-forward tests, the live gate and the switch-off (19.6).
* **The gate** (Arun, 29 Oct 2026):

    * for a holding, the Forecaster appears in weekday cards and chat answers only after 8 live weeks beating both the base rate and the rules engine;
    * until then it appears only on the holding's Forecast tab and in the Saturday review, labelled "in practice";
    * when a holding has no clear view, the card says "no clear view".

* Privacy mode and both themes apply. Charts are in the house style (MA-56): Swift Charts in the app, lightweight-charts in the web app.
* Before it starts, these are drawn (the trace row's undrawn list):

    * 1a's remaining states: loading, stale prices, no Forecaster yet, switched off, an error, a long holding name, AX5 and Reduce Motion;
    * the Forecast tab of a holding with no clear view;
    * each row's sheet;
    * the proof page on iPad.

* Tests:

    * the card from fixtures;
    * the gate;
    * snapshot tests at iPhone and iPad sizes, and Playwright screenshots of the web page at phone and laptop widths.

* Gates: ruff, pyright, pytest; the iOS gates (MA-166); Playwright.

**Depends on.** F5, F8 (MA-280), the wealth phases 2 to 9 (the dashboard in the app and the web app, from MA-208), MA-15 (phase 13 track record).

Updated 25 Oct 2026 (MA-161): the Telegram card becomes a card in the app's chat; the Forecast screen shows in the iOS app and, after Arun's correction, in the web app. Updated 29 Oct 2026 (MA-281): the app's screens picked (1a), the proof page kept, and the Forecaster in practice also shown on the Forecast tab.

As the owner I want my monthly contributions and rebalancing steered by the risk forecasts, so that the household avoids the deepest falls and still grows steadily.

**Where.** `src/mypaisa_agent/forecaster/strategy/`, the first-of-the-month contribution prompt, the lab (F2).

**Acceptance.**

* **The rules:**

    * contributions always continue;
    * new money tilts away from holdings in a stressed or falling mood, or with a high chance of a deep fall;
    * a trend filter applies;
    * rebalancing happens within bands;
    * a drawdown budget that Arun sets.

* It never says sell everything, it stays ISA-safe, and it only suggests, because MyPaisa cannot trade.
* **The test:**

    * backtested over ten years against buy-and-hold with monthly contributions;
    * it must cut the worst fall and the time spent below a previous high;
    * it must not give up more yearly growth than the trade-off Arun sets.

* **Going live:** it runs in shadow for 8 weeks. Then it shows as the suggested split on the first-of-the-month contribution prompt in the chat, beside the rules engine's.
* Tests:

    * a known-answer backtest;
    * the drawdown budget;
    * never more than a band's worth of change in one month.

* Gates: ruff, pyright, pytest.

**Depends on.** F2, F3.

Updated 25 Oct 2026 (MA-161): "the bot" becomes MyPaisa.

As the developer I want the agent team, how it is configured and approved, how it learns and heals, where its data lives, and the new budgets written down, so that phase 12's stories and the earlier budget stories build on one plan.

**Where.**

* `ARCHITECTURE.md`: new §20; data model items; §5.1, §6.4, §15, §15.1, §16, §17, §18.1, §19.2.
* `PLAN.md`: phase 0 limits, MA-113 and MA-116 bullets, phase 12, the timeline, the risks.
* `TECH_STACK.md`, `README.md`.

**Acceptance.**

* **Budgets:**

    * Claude for agents is £200 a month across prod and dev, by default £160 and £40, each a setting.
    * The Console workspace limits are the hard caps.
    * Credit beyond the plan's $100 is paid by card.
    * AWS and other services get £50 a month.

* **Data:** free sources first (yfinance, Twelve Data's free tier, free websites where their terms allow). EODHD only when the data steward shows a free source cannot serve a holding and the admin approves.
* **§20 records:**

    * the agents and their jobs;
    * the settings store;
    * the admin desk in Telegram and the web (admins only);
    * the learning loop (daily grading, daily calibration within bounds, challengers);
    * self-healing;
    * the S3 and DynamoDB layout;
    * budgets per agent;
    * the order of work.

* Gates: none yet (docs only).

**Depends on.** MA-126 (stacked on its branch).

As the admin I want every behaviour of MyPaisa held as a setting I can see and change, with safe bounds, so that nothing is hard-coded and nothing changes without a trace.

**Where.** `src/mypaisa_agent/settings/`, `config/defaults.yaml`, the `Setting` and `ConfigVersion` items; `ARCHITECTURE.md` §20.2.

**Acceptance.**

* **What each setting has:**

    * a key, a value and a schema (type, allowed range);
    * an owner (admin) and an automatic band, inside which agents may move it;
    * an approval rule, a version and a reason.

* **Which settings exist:**

    * data source chains;
    * model and rule parameters;
    * thresholds, horizons and schedules;
    * alert budgets, budget ceilings and agent shares;
    * the learning loop's bounds.

* **Defaults** come from `config/defaults.yaml`, validated in CI.
* **Every change** is a new `ConfigVersion` row and an audit row, made through one settings service that enforces schema, range and approval.
* **Secrets** stay in SSM, set by hand; a setting can only name one. Infrastructure stays in Terraform.
* **Reading settings:** Lambdas read them through a cache of 60 seconds or less, so a change applies within a minute.
* **Snapshots:** a daily snapshot of all settings goes to S3.
* **Tests:**

    * a value out of range is refused;
    * an agent changing a setting outside its band is refused;
    * history replays;
    * the cache picks up a change.

* Gates: ruff, pyright, pytest.

**Depends on.** none.

Updated 25 Oct 2026 (MA-161): "the bot" becomes MyPaisa.

As the admin I want one place, in my chat in the app and in the Admin center (in the app and in the web app), where the agents ask me what they need and ask approval for changes, and where I can tell them what to change.

**Where.** `src/mypaisa_agent/agents/admin_desk.py`, the cards (MA-174), `ios/MyPaisa/` (the Admin center's Approvals, Settings, Agents, Incidents), `web/` (the web Admin center's Approvals, Settings, Agents and Incidents pages, MA-184), the `ChangeRequest`, `AdminQuestion` and `Incident` items; `ARCHITECTURE.md` §20.3. Designed first, drawn for iOS before it starts.

**Acceptance.**

* **Who:** the admin only: the owner, the household's one admin (Arun, 25 Oct 2026: "there is only 1 admin"). No role makes a second admin. Members and viewers never see the desk.
* **What an agent's change request shows:**

    * what would change, from and to;
    * why, with the evidence;
    * the cost and the budget left;
    * the risk, and how to undo it;
    * when the request expires;
    * for a change to a prompt, a rule, a threshold or a model, the affected eval suites' results, with a failing safety case blocking the approval; and, for a proposal drawn from uploaded or fetched text, where it came from (MA-256; still to draw, in the row's undrawn list).

* **Deciding a request:**

    * In the chat it arrives as a card with \[Approve\] \[Reject\] \[Why?\] \[Later\], and by push. In the Admin center it appears on the Approvals screen.
    * Approving needs the second check (MA-76).
    * Either place decides, and the other updates.

* **Questions:** agents can ask with buttons or free text, and the answer resumes their work.
* **Instructions from the admin:** "put Twelve Data first for London", "lower dev to $30". The chat turns each into the same request, shows a Confirm card, and needs the second check.
* **Limiting noise:**

    * non-urgent requests wait for one daily digest;
    * incidents arrive at once;
    * a rejected request is not proposed again for the period in its setting.

* Every decision is audited.
* **Tests:**

    * a member cannot reach the desk;
    * approval without the second check is refused;
    * an expired request does nothing;
    * deciding in one place updates the other;
    * a request with a failing safety case cannot be approved.

* Gates: ruff, pyright, pytest; the iOS gates (MA-166); Playwright for the web Admin center's pages.

**Depends on.** E1, MA-102, MA-76.

Updated 25 Oct 2026 (MA-161): the admin desk is in the app's chat and Admin center, and in the web app's Admin center (a read-only page, plus the actions behind the second check, MA-184); the owner's Telegram chat becomes the owner's chat in the app, and Face ID or PIN in Telegram and the browser's fresh check become the second check (MA-76), in the app and in the web app.

Updated 28 Oct 2026 (MA-245, review round 1): a change request carries its eval results, carried by MA-256 (ARCHITECTURE 23.5).

As the admin I want data to come from yfinance, Twelve Data's free tier and free websites, with EODHD only when it is truly needed, and I want to approve it when it is.

**Where.** `src/mypaisa_agent/agents/data_steward.py`, the price chains (§5.1), `ARCHITECTURE.md` §19.2 and §20.

**Acceptance.**

* **Every feed is watched for:**

    * freshness;
    * gaps;
    * disagreement between sources beyond a tolerance;
    * free-tier quota left.

* **What it does without asking:**

    * fails over within the configured chain;
    * retries;
    * fills gaps on the next run;
    * labels stale data.

* **Free websites** (fund manager pages, exchange pages) are used only where their terms allow, and the terms are recorded.
* **When a holding cannot be served** by free sources for a set number of days, the steward:

    * tries EODHD's free key for that holding;
    * files a change request with the evidence, the price (£19.99 a month for Historian) and the headroom left in the £50 AWS and others line.

* EODHD is never switched on without approval, and its key is put in SSM by hand.
* **Paid feeds are reviewed:** a paid feed that has not been needed for 60 days is proposed for cancellation.
* **Tests:**

    * failover;
    * a gap filled;
    * a disagreement flagged;
    * a paid feed proposed and refused without approval.

* Gates: ruff, pyright, pytest.

**Depends on.** E1, E2, MA-11.

As the owner I want every call and forecast kept exactly as it was made and checked against the real price movements every day, so that the system learns from evidence and its track record grows over the years.

**Where.** The `mypaisa-<env>-lake` bucket (`predictions/`, `outcomes/`), the `ScoreDaily` item, `src/mypaisa_agent/learning/grader.py`; `ARCHITECTURE.md` §20.4 and §20.6.

**Acceptance.**

* **Stored as made:** every stance, forecast, alert and range is written to S3 in Parquet, partitioned by model and date. It carries:

    * its inputs' known date;
    * its model and settings versions;
    * its horizon.

* **Graded daily:** after the close, every prediction whose horizon has passed is graded. The outcome records:

    * the realised return;
    * hit or miss;
    * Brier score and log loss;
    * whether the outcome fell inside the forecast range.

* **Summarised daily:** a `ScoreDaily` row per model, holding and market mood, with rolling 30, 90 and 365-day scores.
* Nothing is ever overwritten. A late price correction adds a regraded outcome, not an edit.
* The Activity page and the Saturday review read the scores.
* **Tests:**

    * grading on fixtures;
    * no prediction is graded before its horizon;
    * a late correction is handled;
    * a ten-year replay through the lab gives the same scores.

* Gates: ruff, pyright, pytest.

**Depends on.** E1, MA-120 (the lab).

Updated 28 Oct 2026 (MA-245): daily on purpose. Its ground truth, the day's prices, is new every day, so it scores outcomes as they arrive; it is not an offline eval on a timer, which ARCHITECTURE 23 rules out (23.6).

As the owner I want the system to keep trying better models and better rule settings, and to switch only when one has proved itself, with my approval.

**Where.** `src/mypaisa_agent/learning/challengers.py`, the `ModelVersion` registry, the lab (MA-120); `ARCHITECTURE.md` §20.4.

**Acceptance.**

* **Challengers:**

    * the monthly retrain;
    * other parameter settings;
    * changes to the rule thresholds within their ranges.

* Each challenger is tested walk-forward over ten years, then run live in shadow beside the current one.
* **Promotion:** a challenger is proposed through the admin desk only when it beats the current one:

    * in the held-out years;
    * over a configured number of live weeks (8 by default);
    * by more than a margin.

* **The proposal shows:**

    * the scores side by side;
    * the worst fall and calibration;
    * what changes for the household.

* Nothing is promoted without approval.
* **After promotion:** the old version stays ready. If the promoted one slips within its probation, the old one is restored on its own.
* **Tests:**

    * a challenger that does not beat the current one is never proposed;
    * promotion needs approval;
    * automatic restore.

* Gates: ruff, pyright, pytest.

**Depends on.** E4, E5, MA-123.

As the admin I want failures fixed without me where that is safe, and brought to me with a clear question where it is not.

**Where.**

* `src/mypaisa_agent/agents/ops_warden.py`;
* a Step Functions state machine for the daily pipeline;
* the EventBridge bus `mypaisa-<env>-events`;
* the `Incident` item;
* `ARCHITECTURE.md` §20.5.

**Acceptance.**

* **The daily pipeline** is one state machine, with retries and backoff on every step, and idempotent writes:

    * ingest;
    * engine;
    * Forecaster;
    * grade;
    * calibrate;
    * brief.

* **Fixed without asking:**

    * a failed step is retried;
    * a step that still fails is skipped, with its output labelled stale;
    * a bad model or calibration is rolled back to its last good version;
    * a missed schedule is caught up.

* **What needs the admin:** anything else opens an `Incident` on the admin desk, with what happened, what was tried and the choices.
* **Every agent reports a heartbeat.** A silent agent is an incident.
* **The 07:45 dead man's switch stays.**
* **Tests:**

    * injected failures at each step;
    * recovery is idempotent;
    * an incident is opened when retries run out.

* Gates: ruff, pyright, pytest, terraform validate.

**Depends on.** E1, E2, MA-107.

As the admin I want to set how much AWS and Claude may spend and when I am warned, from the Admin center in the iOS app or the web app, and to get each warning in the app, by push and by email, so that nothing runs away and I always hear about it.

**Arun's words, 24 Oct 2026.** "Claude Spend Budget will be £200, I have Max x20 plan which give me that. Threshold to be set and kept configurable which I can control from Web portal Admin. 1. AWS capping 2. Claude cost capping. 3. Get Alerts on my web app, telegram and if possible email too (if free)."

**Where.**

* the Admin center in both doors: `ios/MyPaisa/` (Spend and the ceiling, Caps and alerts, Alert routing) and `web/` (the web app's Spend and Alerts pages, MA-184);
* the `api` admin routes;
* `BudgetSettings`, `AwsSpendMonth`, `AlertRule` and `Alert`;
* an SNS topic `mypaisa-<env>-admin-alerts`;
* AWS Budgets in `infra/`;
* `ARCHITECTURE.md` §15.2.

Designed first: the boards of `iPhone admin.dc.html` for the iOS app, and the picked browser boards in MyPaisa app v1 for the web app.

**Acceptance.**

* **Claude caps, all settings:**

    * the ceiling per environment, in dollars, by default $160 prod and $40 dev: the Max 20x plan's $200 monthly credit;
    * the steps (70%, 85%, 95%, 100% by default; any ascending list);
    * the member share, the daily guard and the calls per run;
    * each agent's share, set here once MA-136 (phase 18) brings the agents (PLAN E8);
    * the Console hard limit, typed in to match.
    * A ceiling that would pass the credit warns that the extra needs a card, and needs an explicit yes.

* **AWS caps, all settings:**

    * the monthly cap for AWS and others, £50 by default;
    * alert steps (50%, 80%, 100% by default);
    * an alert when the month's forecast passes the cap;
    * what happens at each step: by default, dev's schedules pause at 80%; at 100%, dev pauses and so do non-essential prod jobs (back-fills, retraining). The brief, alerts and chat never stop.
    * Actions are flags the jobs read, never changes to AWS resources.

* **Metering AWS:**

    * once a day from Cost Explorer: month to date by service and by `env` tag, plus the forecast;
    * about $0.01 a request;
    * AWS's figures lag up to a day, and the screen says so.

* **The backstop (Terraform, not set from the Admin center):**

    * one AWS Budgets cost budget at the hard limit (£75 by default, in dollars), alerting the SNS topic on actual and forecast;
    * a budget action that stops dev's schedules;
    * monitoring and up to two action-enabled budgets are free.
    * Raising it is a pull request.

* **Alerts:**

    * one router with a matrix by alert type (Claude spend, AWS spend, incident - including the day-60 TestFlight build-age alert from MA-167, approval, data, holding) and severity (info, warning, critical), each with:

        * the app: the Alerts page in the Admin center with a badge, in the iOS app and in the web app, and a card in the admin's chat;
        * push: critical is time-sensitive with sound (MA-170);
        * email.

    * quiet hours per channel, with critical able to break through;
    * a cooldown, and a daily digest option.

* **Email** goes through Amazon SNS. The first 1,000 email notifications a month are free.

    * The address is put in SSM by hand, and Terraform makes the subscription; it is confirmed once (see Open).
    * Email carries system alerts only, never holding values, unless the admin turns that on.

* **Every change** to a cap, step, action or route needs the second check (MA-76): in the iOS app a fresh Sign in with Apple or passkey under Face ID (a member who signs in only with Google proves it with Google again), in the web app a passkey or a fresh sign-in in the browser. Each writes an audit row and sends a message to the admin's chat and a push.
* **Tests:**

    * each step fires once per cycle;
    * an action pauses the right jobs and never the brief;
    * the routing matrix (including the day-60 TestFlight build-age warning alert);
    * quiet hours;
    * the SNS publish;
    * a Claude ceiling past the credit needs the explicit yes;
    * snapshot tests of the Spend and Alerts screens, and Playwright screenshots of the web pages.

